    <script type="application/ld+json">{"mainEntity":{"headline":"Power failure: why small sample size undermines the reliability of neuroscience","description":"\n                \n                \n                  \n                \n                \n                  \n                \n                \n                  \n                \n                \n                  \n                \n                \n                  \n                \n                \n                  \n                \n                \n                  \n                \n              \n               Low-powered studies lead to overestimates of effect size and low reproducibility of results. In this Analysis article, Munafò and colleagues show that the average statistical power of studies in the neurosciences is very low, discuss ethical implications of low-powered studies and provide recommendations to improve research practices. A study with low statistical power has a reduced chance of detecting a true effect, but it is less well appreciated that low power also reduces the likelihood that a statistically significant result reflects a true effect. Here, we show that the average statistical power of studies in the neurosciences is very low. The consequences of this include overestimates of effect size and low reproducibility of results. There are also ethical dimensions to this problem, as unreliable research is inefficient and wasteful. Improving reproducibility in neuroscience is a key priority and requires attention to well-established but often ignored methodological principles.","datePublished":"2013-04-10T00:00:00Z","dateModified":"2013-04-15T00:00:00Z","pageStart":"365","pageEnd":"376","sameAs":"https://doi.org/10.1038/nrn3475","keywords":["Molecular neuroscience","Biomedicine","general","Neurosciences","Behavioral Sciences","Biological Techniques","Neurobiology","Animal Genetics and Genomics"],"image":["https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/i